      "title": "آپ کیسے ہیں؟ / آپ کیسی ہیں؟ (āp kaise haiṅ? / āp kaisī haiṅ?) — ask respectfully",
      "headword": "آپ کیسے ہیں؟ / آپ کیسی ہیں؟",
      "romanization": "āp kaise haiṅ? / āp kaisī haiṅ?",
      "gloss": "How are you? — respectfully to a man / woman",
      "script": "urdu-nastaliq",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:346cac317c82c911",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Choose respectful āp. Then choose kaise for a man or kaisī for a woman. One final copula completes the question."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "The exchange",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "to a man: آپ کیسے ہیں؟ — āp kaise haiṅ? to a woman: آپ کیسی ہیں؟ — āp kaisī haiṅ?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The order is you + how + are? Keep ہیں haiṅ final, just as ہے hai stayed final in the name question. Respectful āp takes this plural-shaped copula even when speaking to one person."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: respect does not erase agreement",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both lines are respectful. The choice between kaise and kaisī tracks the person addressed, not greater or lesser politeness. If you do not know how a person wishes to be addressed, listen for their usage or ask rather than guess; the grammar lesson does not replace social judgment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "to a man — āp kaise haiṅ?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: to a man — **āp kaise haiṅ?**]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "to a woman — āp kaisī haiṅ?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: to a woman — **āp kaisī haiṅ?**]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "KEEP",
              "instruction": "respectful āp and final haiṅ in both",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU KEEP: respectful **āp** and final **haiṅ** in both]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What stays the same in both lines? (Āp... haiṅ.) What changes? (Kaise / kaisī.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sources: Basic Urdu: Formal Conversation and Personal Pronouns with “To Be”."
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "UR-C04-aap-kaise-hain-man",
              "prompt": "Type the respectful Urdu question 'How are you?' addressed to a man.",
              "assesses": [
